 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Yes. On advertised standard variable rates, investors pay more than owner-occupiers, and the gap has held at 0.58 percentage points.\n\n**September 2026, the latest data:**\n- Owner-occupier standard variable: 8.77%\n- Investor standard variable: 9.35%\n- Gap: 0.58 percentage points\n\n**The gap over time:** It was 0.58 points in January 2025 (8.77% against 9.35%). It stayed at that level through every month since, including the rate falls in 2025 and the rises in 2026. Both rates fell to 8.02% and 8.60% from August 2025. They then rose back to 8.77% and 9.35% by May 2026, and haven't moved since.\n\n**Caveat:** These are the RBA's advertised indicator rates. Most borrowers pay less than that because of discounts, and the discounted variable rates are lower than the standard ones. I haven't checked whether the investor gap is the same for the rates borrowers actually pay (RBA table F6) or for discounted loans.\n\nSource: RBA F5 Indicator Lending Rates.",
